{"causes": ["The ongoing Western Schism, which divided the Catholic Church between competing papal claimants, necessitated the involvement of high-ranking clergy in its resolution.", "The political influence and social standing of the noble Caracciolo family of Naples provided Corrado with avenues for advancement within the Church hierarchy.", "The widespread desire among European rulers and clergy for an end to the papal schism created a climate where conciliar movements gained significant support."], "effects": ["Corrado Caracciolo's participation in the Council of Pisa in 1409 contributed to the election of Antipope Alexander V, further complicating the Western Schism by creating three papal claimants.", "His elevation to cardinal by the Pisan line solidified his alignment with the conciliar movement, which sought to resolve papal disputes through general councils.", "His administrative and ecclesiastical activities helped sustain the Pisan obedience within the Catholic Church, albeit temporarily, until its eventual decline."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "western-schism", "sourceName": "Western Schism", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "corrado-caracciolo", "targetName": "Corrado Caracciolo", "context": "The Western Schism profoundly shaped Corrado Caracciolo's ecclesiastical career and compelled his involvement in efforts to restore Church unity from 1378 to 1417."}, {"sourceSlug": "corrado-caracciolo", "sourceName": "Corrado Caracciolo", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "naples", "targetName": "Naples, Italy", "context": "Corrado Caracciolo was born in Naples around 1350 and began his early ecclesiastical career within the Kingdom of Naples."}, {"sourceSlug": "corrado-caracciolo", "sourceName": "Corrado Caracciolo", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "council-of-pisa", "targetName": "Council of Pisa", "context": "Corrado Caracciolo was an active participant in the Council of Pisa in 1409, where he was elevated to cardinal and supported the election of a new pope."}, {"sourceSlug": "corrado-caracciolo", "sourceName": "Corrado Caracciolo", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "antipope-alexander-v", "targetName": "Antipope Alexander V", "context": "Corrado Caracciolo collaborated with Antipope Alexander V, who elevated him to cardinal at the Council of Pisa in 1409."}, {"sourceSlug": "corrado-caracciolo", "sourceName": "Corrado Caracciolo", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "catholic-church", "targetName": "Catholic Church", "context": "Corrado Caracciolo's administrative roles and his support for the conciliar movement influenced the internal dynamics of the Catholic Church during the Western Schism."}], "places": [{"name": "Naples, Italy", "role": "Birthplace, early career"}, {"name": "Pisa, Italy", "role": "Site of Council"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Place of death"}], "texts": [], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1604964"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Corrado Caracciolo held a significant position as a cardinal during the Western Schism, actively participating in the pivotal Council of Pisa (1409) and contributing to the complex, albeit initially unsuccessful, efforts to resolve the papal crisis.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-23T07:03:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39911408890", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Corrado Caracciolo (b. 1350, Naples) was a entrepreneur or business leader of the Medieval period, shaped by feudalism, religious institutions, and cultural exchange, active in Naples. Known in the fields of religious figures.", "newValue": "Corrado Caracciolo (b. 1350, Naples – d. 1411-02-24, Rome) was a prominent Italian cardinal and administrator whose ecclesiastical career unfolded during the tumultuous period of the Western Schism. Hailing from the influential Caracciolo family of Naples, his life and decisions were inextricably linked to the struggle for papal legitimacy that fractured the Catholic Church for nearly four decades. His ascent through the ranks of the Church, marked by appointments as Bishop of Monopoli and later Archbishop of Mileto, positioned him at the heart of the political and religious machinations that defined late medieval Europe.

Caracciolo's most significant involvement came with his participation in the Council of Pisa in 1409. This council, convened by cardinals from both the Roman and Avignon obediences, aimed to resolve the schism by deposing both Pope Gregory XII (Roman) and Antipope Benedict XIII (Avignon) and electing a new, universally recognized pontiff. Caracciolo, having been elevated to cardinal by Antipope Alexander V at Pisa, actively supported this third, Pisan line of popes. His actions reflected the widespread desire for Church unity, even as the Pisan solution inadvertently complicated matters by introducing a third claimant to the papacy.

His legacy is thus tied to the prolonged and ultimately unsuccessful efforts to heal the Western Schism through conciliar means prior to the more decisive Council of Constance. While the Council of Pisa failed to achieve its immediate goal of unification, Caracciolo’s administrative contributions and his steadfast support for the conciliar movement represented a significant current within the Church hierarchy that sought reform and resolution. His career exemplifies the profound challenges faced by churchmen navigating a period of unprecedented internal division, where loyalty to a particular pope often clashed with the broader imperative of Christian unity."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-23T07:03:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39911408890", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1350, Naples"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1350, Naples"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Western Schism, which divided the Catholic Church between competing papal claimants, necessitated the involvement of high-ranking clergy in its resolution.", "The political influence and social standing of the noble Caracciolo family of Naples provided Corrado with avenues for advancement within the Church hierarchy.", "The widespread desire among European rulers and clergy for an end to the papal schism created a climate where conciliar movements gained significant support."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-23T07:03:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39911408890", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1411-02-24"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1411-02-24"}], "newValue": ["Corrado Caracci